# L §4.6 step 3.2 (round 11b): "the narrower row: at every key position its key is the other's or lies inside it (a
# literal inside a range that holds it ...), and at one position strictly inside. So ... `[2026]` beats `[2025..2029]`".
# x[3] is the narrower row at key 3, so it wins there; at key 4 only x[1..5] is a candidate. The rows state their keys
# differently, so they are not "at the same keys" (step 2) and v2 needs no replace.
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"

index ns = 1..10
input x[n in ns]: int

version v1
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2025-12-01
  x[1..5] = 2

version v2
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-02-01
  x[3] = 1

ask q3: x[3]
ask q4: x[4]
=== expect
q3: 1
  cut 2026-12-31
q4: 2
  cut 2026-12-31
